//! MUST NOT COMPILE.
//!
//! A path to a payload field is two segments spliced together: body root down
//! to `execution_payload`, then payload root down to the field. `concat` takes
//! the outer segment first, and the two orders give different nodes.
//!
//! Getting this backwards is an easy mistake to make and an invisible one to
//! review: both orders are plausible-looking constants, and a verifier built
//! on the wrong one proves a node nobody cares about while looking perfectly
//! healthy. Lido's and EigenLayer's equivalents are hand-written constants
//! with no way to express the relationship at all.
//!
//! Here the relationship is written down, so the build fails and the
//! diagnostic prints both numbers.

// NOTE: the field counts below are illustrative, not a layout source. The
// per-fork table has not yet been verified against the consensus specs, so
// nothing in this repository treats these numbers as authoritative. What this
// case demonstrates -- that joining two segments in the wrong order yields a
// different node, and that the mistake is caught at compile time -- does not
// depend on the counts being right.

use verife::gindex::{concat, field_gindex}

/// `execution_payload` is field 9 of an 11-field Capella `BeaconBlockBody`.
const BODY_TO_PAYLOAD: u64 = field_gindex(field: 9, field_count: 11)

/// `block_hash` is field 12 of a 15-field Capella `ExecutionPayload`.
const PAYLOAD_TO_BLOCK_HASH: u64 = field_gindex(field: 12, field_count: 15)

/// The body root down to `block_hash`: the payload segment spliced under the
/// body segment.
const CORRECT: u64 = concat(outer: BODY_TO_PAYLOAD, inner: PAYLOAD_TO_BLOCK_HASH)

/// The same two segments, joined the other way round.
const SWAPPED: u64 = concat(outer: PAYLOAD_TO_BLOCK_HASH, inner: BODY_TO_PAYLOAD)

// Asserting that the two agree is asserting that the order does not matter.
// It does, so this fails -- and the diagnostic names both gindices.
static_assert(SWAPPED == CORRECT)
